\par\par \b \uc2  FULL TEXT-Bank of Thailand statement on baht. \b0\par\par\uc2 764 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, July 2 (Reuter) - Following is the full text of the statement by the Bank of Thailand on Wednesday on changes to the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  1. The Minister of Finance, upon the recommendation of the Bank of Thailand, on 2 July 1997 issued a Notification determining that the value of the baht would henceforth be set by market forces. \par \par \uc2  2. As from 2 November 1984, the baht value had been set in reference to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Thailand's major trading partners. The exchange rate of the baht against the United States dollar was announced daily by the Exchange Equalization Fund, which stood ready to buy and sell US dollars with commercial banks at determined rates. This system resulted in the baht becoming one of the most stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world and supported Thailand's rapid growth over the years. \par \par \uc2  3. The tight monetary policy followed by the Bank of Thailand since 1995 in order to contain incipient pressures on inflation and the current account have had the desired effect on economic stability. However, the coincidental sharp slowdown of exports and capital inflows in 1996, together with problems in financial institutions resulted in a rapid deceleration of investment and growth. Calls for a relaxation of monetary policy evoked widespread speculation that exchange rate policy might be adjusted to allow for reductions in interest rates. While the authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were successful in containing speculative pressure in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the confidence of domestic businesses in the authorities' exchange rate policy continued to weaken in the wake of widespread speculation and criticisms. \par \par \uc2  4. In order to end uncertainty over Thailand's exchange rate policy, Thailand's exchange rate system will from 2 July 1997 be a managed float, whereby the value of the baht will be determined by market forces to reflect economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  5. Under the new system, movements in the exchange rate are expected to be quite large at the initial stage. However, relative stability should be attained after a brief adjustment period. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervence\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets from time to time to achieve policy objectives and guard against excessive fiuctuations. \par \par \uc2  6. To support the new \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, a cautious monetary policy stance will continue to be followed. Effective today, the Bank of Thailand has raised the bank rate from 10.5 to 12.5 percent. In due course, the Bank will allow market forces to adjust interest rates downward in the context of a stable or strengthening baht. In the longer run, the managed float system will enable the authorities to implement monetary policy consistent with domestic policy objectives and support healthy capital inflows. \par \par \uc2  7. Some slowdown in economic activity must be accepted in the wake of the new exchange rate regime. However, this will lay the groundwork for growth with stability in the years ahead. In the short run, so as to alleviate any negative effect on debt servicing and prices that may result from an adjustment in the baht value, the authorities are prepared to consider implementing supplementary measures, particularly those related to prices of goods considered as necessities, tax deduction for businesses suffering exchange losses, and financing facilities for priority economic sectors. \par \par \uc2  8. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of Thailand believe that the managed float system will benefit Thailand's overall economic development. The new system will provide sufficient flexibility to accommodate developments in Thailand's economy and respond to the situation in financial and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  ---------------- Notification of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  By virtue of the power conferred upon him under section 8 Paragraph Three of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act, B.E. 2501, as amended by The \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act (No.4) B.E. 2516. \par \par \uc2  The Minister of Finance, upon the recommendation of the Bank of Thailand, hereby issues this notification as follows; \par \par \uc2  1. The Notification of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  dated 2 November B.E. 2527 Re; Change of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  System is hereby repealed. \par \par \uc2  2. The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  system shall be such that the value of the Baht is set by conditions in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  3. The Bank of Thailand shall engage in buying and selling of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the purpose of maintaining the stability of the Baht under the system mentioned in 2. \par \par \uc2  4. The Bank of Thailand shall from time to time as deemed necessary, publish the exchange rates between the Baht and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for use as a reference rate in conversion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  into Baht. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt7202zmy