\par\par \b \uc2  Thailand/Floats Baht/Amplifier -4: Recession Likely In '97 \b0\par\par\uc2 340 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 1997\par 16:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The floating of the baht is likely to result in deep cuts in forecasts for economic growth, including those of the Bank of Thailand. Rerngchai said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut its forecast for 5.9% growth this year. Saker of SocGen-Crosby said he expects the Thai economy to shrink in the half of 1997, fulfilling the standard definition of a recession. For the full year, he said economic growth will be 1%. Thailand hasn't had a recession in 30 years. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand acknowledged a slowdown is inevitable, although it said nothing of recession. In a statement it said: 'The authorities are prepared to consider implementing supplementary measures, particularly those related to prices of goods considered necessities, tax deductions for businesses suffering exchange losses, and financing facilities for priority economic sectors.' \par \par \uc2  The Thai stock market, however, is likely to benefit. Thailand's benchmark stock index had fallen 36% since the start of the year to 527.28 points before the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float. That's the amount Mexico's index fell in reaction to the peso devaluation. Because Thailand's stock market anticipated the baht's fall, stock prices are now likely to move up, analysts said. The Stock Exchange of Thailand index Wednesday registered its biggest single-day gain in nine months, climbing 41.51 points to 568.79 points. \par \par \uc2  Under the new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the baht only 'from time to time to achieve policy objectives and guard against excessive fluctuations.' \par \par \uc2  That means scrapping the trading band that obligated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  whenever the dollar moved more than 0.08% in one day. It also means scrapping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket -consisting of the dollar, the Deutsche mark and the Japanese yen- to which the baht had been pegged since 1984. \par \par \uc2  -James Hookway and Warangkana Tempati of Dow Jones Newswires contributed to this article. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 02-07-97 \par \par \uc2  1401GMT \par \par \uc2  1195 4221 62557 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt7204rdo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
