\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - Regionals calm, baht awaits data. \b0\par\par\uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 1997\par 07:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 26 (Reuter) - The Thai baht was steady on Thursday ahead of the release of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monthly economic data expected later in the session, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment was cautious as the market waits to see the extent to which \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserve declined after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the baht from speculation last month. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand will announce April and May economic data at 0800 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a sharp fall in reserves would put downward pressure on the baht. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Chavalit Yonchaiyudh has said he does not want to devalue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The baht was quoted at 25.840/870 at 0430 GMT to the dollar against 25.820/850 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  But trade was relatively quiet in the onshore market, with comments from former Thai Prime Minister Chatichai Choonhavan in New Delhi having little affect on activity, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Chatichai said the Bank of Thailand estimates that gross domestic product (GDP) will grow by 5.9 percent in 1997, down from 6.4 percent last year. He also said the government would strongly defend the value of the baht. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand kept the baht/dollar midrate unchanged at 25.77. Offshore, the baht was barely changed at 25.400/500 against 25.300/25.400 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit recovered as dollar gains were capped on the topside near the level where dealers felt the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 2.5240. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was well bid in the offshore market but failed to trek beyond 2.5250, which continues to keep ringgit defensive. \par \par \uc2  "People are still trying to buy the dollar/Malay. There is commericial demand for the dollar before June 30, but market is still long dollars offshore," a European brokerage dealer said. \par \par \uc2  He said dollar demand was prompted by mid-year book closing and some window-dressing activities. \par \par \uc2  At 0430 GMT, the ringgit was quoted at 2.5200/100, up from 2.5232/42 at its close in Kuala Lumpur on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Malaysian dealers said there was also some caution as the system could turn tight. Some two billion ringgit from a Bank Negara lending maturity is said to leave the market on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  In Indonesia, the rupiah was stable against the dollar on sufficient liquidity, with short-term swaps easing. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were few market-moving factors and the dollar/rupiah looked trapped within current narrow ranges. \par \par \uc2  Some selling activity was sighted in short-term swaps early in the session, dampened by ample liquidity. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Bank Indonesia, supplied some 262 billion rupiah temporarily in the market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band was kept at 2,424/2,616 and the conversion band at 2,424/2,472. \par \par \uc2  Rupiah traded at 2,431.00/2.00 at 0430 GMT, unchanged from opening levels. \par \par \uc2  Across the Causeway, the Singapore dollar was quoted at 1.4267/77 at 0430 GMT, little changed throughout the morning. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the outlook was for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stay pinned within the 1.4250/1.4300 range. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unaffected by the defacto bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's, raising the lending limit to offshore banks to S$200 million from S$150 million on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  -- Syed Azman in Kuala Lumpur (603-206 2323), Mantik Kusjanto in Jakarta (6221 384-6364), Anuchit Nguyen in Bangkok (662) 652-0642 and Jacqueline Wong in Singapore (65-8703199) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt6q0391c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
