\par\par \b \uc2  Thai  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  defense seen relying on attrition. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nailene Chou Wiest \par \uc2 549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 23 (Reuter) - The standoff between the Thai government and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators is likely to extend over the next four to six weeks in a brutal war of attrition, U.S. trader and hedge fund managers said. \par \par \uc2  In the forward market, the Thai government stands to suffer heavy reserve losses if the baht is devalued before the large positions which it has put on to stem speculative pressure are closed, they said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent about $2 billion to $3 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the spot market, but its contingent liabilities in the forward market are several times larger, estimated a fund manager. \par \par \uc2  "We are half way through the crisis," he said. "I don't think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cave in at this point." \par \par \uc2  Most of the aggressive selling by international hedge funds in April and early May involved contracts over 60-day and 90-day baht forward rates, which eventually prompted the Thai government to impose a two-tier exchange rate system. \par \par \uc2  Hedge funds under the control of George Soros were reported to have $4 billion in forward \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  contracts due in August, according to The Nation newspaper published in Thailand on Monday. A spokesman from Soros Funds in New York, citing company policy, declined comments on the report. \par \par \uc2  The pressure in the next 30 to 45 days is reflected in volatility of the baht forward rates.  Traders quoted one-month rate at 86.8 percent of the spot bid and 96.6 offered. Two-month was bid 50 percent, six-month 31 percent and 12-month 24 percent. \par \par \uc2  In the latest round of baht selling on Finance Minister Amnuay's resignation last week, Thai corporations joined the fray and the battleground was mostly in the spot market, traders said. \par \par \uc2  David Rolley, a portfolio manager at Loomis Sayles in Boston, compared the situation to Mexican corporations selling pesos before the the devaluation in 1994. \par \par \uc2  "It was not just speculators who bet on an overvalued baht," he said, adding that "selling also came from legitimate corporate hedging against their dollar liabilities." \par \par \uc2  The Thai private sector owes $66 billion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt, of which the bulk is due in one year or less, he said. \par \par \uc2  Currnecy traders said concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Southeast Asian central banks stablized the baht at about 25.00 per U.S. dollar and the level appeared to be holding. \par \par \uc2  The forward market, in contrast, was eerily slow, they said. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the upper hand right now," said a trader, noting that quite a few hedge funds were groaning because they were trapped in the market by the exchange control. \par \par \uc2  "It is the most vindictive system we have seen," said an analyst, who asked not to be named. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, hedge funds have mounted some unsuccessful \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  attacks in the past, such as the assault on the French franc, but they could liquidate their positions in the open market and reclaim some value, whereas in Thailand's current two-tier exchange rate system, the speculators had no way out, he said. \par \par \uc2  "When it is over, I doubt anyone would ever try to attack the baht again," the analyst said. \par \par \uc2  (( - 212-859-1674)) \pa