\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Thailand \highlight\uc2 : Baht Lower Early Amid  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 292 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 1997\par 05:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK (AP-Dow Jones)--The Thai baht is down in domestic and offshore spot trading early Friday amid heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as Thai corporations buy dollars to hedge foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, is up slightly in offshore trading. It also has strengthened in the swap market, where investors make agreements to buy and sell baht at specified times in the future. \par \par \uc2  Around 0240 GMT (10:40 p.m. EDT), the dollar was at 25.95 baht in the domestic market, up from 25.93 baht at the close of trading Thursday. In the offshore market, the dollar is at 25.40 baht, up from 25.10 baht at the close of trading Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The domestic rate indicates the dollar is trading far outside upper limit of the band of 25.77 baht to 25.81 baht in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowing it to trade on Friday - evidence that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is selling dollars to commercial banks at below market rates. \par \par \uc2  'There's still a lot of corporate demand for dollars,' a dealer at a Japanese bank said. 'People are afraid the government will devalue the baht.' Some of the country's biggest corporations, including telecommunications companies, are hedging debt as a result. \par \par \uc2  The devaluation fears follow the resignations Thursday of Finance Minister Amnuay Viravan and Commerce Minister Narongchai Akrasanee - moves that appear to leave Thai economic policy adrift as the threat of a recession grows. On Friday, Thai newspapers reported that Thanong Bidaya, president of Thai Military Bank PCL, would replace Amnuay. Commerce's Narongchai, meanwhile, said he'd reversed his decision to resign. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 20-06-97 \par \par \uc2  0314GMT \par \par \uc2  1028 1861 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt6k04j53\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
