\par\par \b \uc2  Thailand/SEC/Short-Selling -2: Can't Do Anything Legally \b0\par\par\uc2 237 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 1997\par 12:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other Southeast Asian central banks brought the dollar down to a six-month low by the end of last week. Speculators were burned as they found themselves unable to unwind their positions because they couldn't get their hands on baht. Some observers estimate they lost up to $300 million. \par \par \uc2  But unlike the Bank of Thailand in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, there's nothing the SEC can legally do to counter short-selling as it takes place outside its jurisdiction. \par \par \uc2  Prasarn declined to comment on whether the SEC action is being taken independently of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But analysts are concerned. \par \par \uc2  'Foreign banks who've been active in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market might want to start dumping Thai stocks to get hold of baht,' said the chief representative of a foreign securities house, who asked not to be named. 'The more the Bank of Thailand strangles the baht, the more foreigners will sell down the stock market.' \par \par \uc2  Observers said the Thai regulators could be sending a warning to foreign investors short-selling in offshore markets that they're being watched. But that situation may not last for long. Prasarn said the Securities & Exchange Commission expects to see its own short-selling regulations come into force by the fourth quarter this year. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 23-05-97 \par \par \uc2  1003GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt5n03pve\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\p