\par\par \b \uc2  Thai stocks in doldrums despite baht defence. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vithoon Amorn \par \uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 1997\par 11:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, May 16 (Reuter) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators retreated to nurse their wounds after Asian central banks fended off attacks on the Thai baht this week, but there was little sign of relief for the country's frail stock market, analysts said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the Thai bourse, rated the worst performer among more than 50 stock exchanges in recent months, may get a brief lift from Thailand's success in defending its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from speculative attacks. But few expected the prolonged downtrend in stocks to bottom soon. \par \par \uc2  "We finally got some good news that the Bank of Thailand succeeded in defending the baht, but most people feel Thailand just won the battle but not the war," said Mike Stead, chief analyst at Union Securities Co Ltd. \par \par \uc2  He said Thai stocks may, at best, stage a small technical rebound in line with the baht. The composite SET Index was down 6.56 points, or 1.18 percent, to 551.31 at 0800 GMT on Friday. \par \par \uc2  At 0800 GMT, the baht was quoted at 25.800/850 to the dollar against 25.300/340 late on Thursday. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  said attacks on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had ended following heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Thai and other regional central banks this week. \par \par \uc2  The baht's losses earlier this week sparked a fall in the country's stocks to near eight-year lows. \par \par \uc2  The SET Index has lost 35 percent in 1996 and 33.7 percent so far this year, including a 16.6 percent setback this month. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said weak economic fundamentals, blamed for hurting market sentiment and helping fuel rumours of policy conflicts in the government this week, remained largely unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The Thai economy is projected to grow at five to six percent this year, its slowest pace in a decade. The country's troubled property sector has sharply weakened bank earnings and pushed some over-exposed finance companies to near bankruptcy. \par \par \uc2  Economist Somjai Phagaphasvivat of Thammasat University said the government was walking an economic tight-rope. \par \par \uc2  "Thailand's dilemma is it could not lower high domestic interest rates to help revive the economy and stock market because doing it now would further weaken the baht and trigger a massive capital flight," he said. \par \par \uc2  Somjai said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had few options but to maintain its high interest rate and baht fixing policies until money and stock markets gained more stability. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand said on Friday that measures would be announced on Monday to help support the stock market, including a bond issue to raise funds to purchase margin loans from securities companies. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the market's mood remained cautious and reflected lingering jitters about the economy and the disastrous first quarter earnings of most listed firms. \par \par \uc2  "We don't know if some more bad news (is) lurking around the corner," a foreign analyst said. \par \par \uc2  Stead of Union Securities said Thai energy stocks and some electronics issues stood out as the best bargains for investors still with the nerves to return to the market. \par \par \uc2  He said Electricity Generating Plc, Cogeneration Plc, and Banpu Plc enjoyed strong earnings potential and could quickly rebound from their recent steep losses tied to the bearish market. \par \par \uc2  "I am not particularly optimistic on banks and especially finance companies. Any bank rebound will be limited," he said. \par \par \uc2  The bank and finance sectors, hurt by bad loans to property developers, have tumbled 31.5 and 58.5 percent so far this year. In March, the government ordered a revamp of the finance sector. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt5g05hh0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
