\par\par \b \uc2  Thailand/Dlr/Baht -2: Dollar Seen Moving Higher \b0\par\par\uc2 208 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 1997\par 04:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK (AP-Dow Jones)--The Thai baht opened lower against the dollar in Asian spot trading Thursday as investers continued to dump the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on fears of devaluation and amid growing uncertainty over the country's economic management, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0135 GMT (9:35 p.m. EDT), the dollar is at 25.985 baht, up from 26.30 baht at the close of trading Wednesday and well above the upper limit of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band, indicating brisk dollar-buying, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set its daily midrate for the dollar at 25.81 baht Thursday. Because of that midrate, the band in which the bank allows the dollar to trade against the baht has been set between 25.79 baht and 25.83 baht Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the dollar to move higher as investors, nervous about the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation and the change in the country's economic policy, continued to sell baht in favor of dollars. \par \par \uc2  'The baht is still very vulnerable today,' said a dealer at a U.S. bank in Bangkok. 'I think we can see the dollar rising further.' \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 15-05-97 \par \par \uc2  0212GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt5f03jq4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
