\par\par \b \uc2  Thailand:  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Rules Out Baht Devaluation \b0\par\par\uc2 156 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 1997\par 08:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK (AP-Dow Jones)--Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday ruled out a baht devaluation or any change in the country's exchange-rate system and urged Thai companies and investors not to lose confidence in the system. \par \par \uc2  'The bank would like to ask the private sector to remain confident in the authority's policy and to ignore rumors because it will only support the action of speculators who want to sabotage the confidence and the security of the country,' the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been under attack by speculators since Wednesday, although \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore have helped forestall further decline in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The baht fell Wednesday to its lowest point in more than seven years, as the dollar closed at 26.30 baht. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 15-05-97 \par \par \uc2  0643GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt5f03e03\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
