\par\par \b \uc2  Economists rule out Thai baht devaluation. [Corrected 5:57 gmt] \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christopher Pizzey \par \uc2 568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 15 (Reuter) - There is no reason why the Thai baht should be devalued, economists said on Thursday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered in Asia after Wednesday's sell-off. \par \par \uc2  "The economic fundamentals are with the Bank of Thailand. The current account deficit is collapsing. There's no reason for a devaluation," Desmond Supple, Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist (corrects title) at BZW in Singapore told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Lawrence Hathaway, Chief Economist East Asia at UBS Securities in Singapore agreed. "There are sound reasons that devaluation is not unambiguously good for the economy." \par \par \uc2  At 0322 gmt the baht was quoted against the dollar at 25.80/90. Traders said there was little activity because of the huge dealing spread. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the baht hit a low around 26.26, prompting joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Market talk also had it that other Asian central banks, such as the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also interevened to defend the baht, but there was no confirmation. \par \par \uc2  The baht has come under speculative pressure in the last couple of days on growing concern about the slowing Thai economy and rumours of political turmoil within the Thai cabinet. \par \par \uc2  There were also rumours that Finance Minister Amnay Viravan could resign, although he has denied this. \par \par \uc2  BZW's Supple said the Bank of Thailand was fighting the speculators by pushing short-term rates sharply higher. "The implied overnight rate is at 150 percent right now. They're making the speculators pay," he said. \par \par \uc2  By boosting short-term interest rates central banks can make it hugely expensive for speculators to short a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Thailand's economic policies are working, but the market is trying them on," Supple said. \par \par \uc2  He said Thailand had been very successful in reducing the current account deficit. The deficit was now around six percent of Gross Domestic Product (GDP), compared with 8.2 percent last year, he said. \par \par \uc2  "If you look at the three month moving average the current account deficit was 18 billion baht in February compared with 37 billion in February last year," Supple said. \par \par \uc2  "The people forecasting devaluation are assuming the current account deficit will not come down and that's just not the case," he said. \par \par \uc2  Hathaway of UBS commented; "The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very committed to maintaining the current baht peg. There's been no wavering at the political level." \par \par \uc2  As Hathaway spoke to Reuters, Amnuay was in Tokyo telling an international conference, "We are determined to fight speculation. I repeat we are determined to fight speculation." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand also told investors in a statement not to listen to rumours of a devaluation. It said its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was meant to protect against speculation on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Hathaway said that to protect the economy, "The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be reluctant to boost interest rates for a prolonged period of time." \par \par \uc2  But with short-term rates moving up so sharply, "It underscores how expensive it is to short the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The head of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at a European bank in Singapore agreed. \par \par \uc2  Referring to the rise in short-term rates, he said, "They are not going to devalue the baht and they'll go to any lengths to avoid it." \par \par \uc2  -- Singapore newsroom (65) 870-3080 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt5f05mqb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
