\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of Thailand/Baht Rumors -3: Swap Rates Drop \b0\par\par\uc2 205 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 1997\par 06:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers in Singapore confirmed that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has moved to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in both spot and swap markets Friday morning. Some said the bank sold dollars in the swap market, hoping that the increase in supply will help lower the swap premiums and reduce pressure on the baht. At the same time, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also bought the baht to boost demand, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  'The offers (to buy baht) like rocks from people who usually deal for the Bank of Thailand,' said a baht dealer in Singapore, who declined to be named. Because of that, swap premiums are dropping, dealers said. At 0415 GMT (12:15 a.m. EDT Friday), the six-month swap premiums are at 37.5-38.5 satang, down from 38.0-38.5 satang quoted earlier in the morning session. \par \par \uc2  The spot rate is also dropping as demand for baht mounts, dealers said. At 0415 GMT, the dollar is at 26.013 baht, below 26.053 baht at the close of trading Thursday and also down from 26.021 baht at 0250 GMT (10:50 p.m. EDT Thursday). \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 09-05-97 \par \par \uc2  0442GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt5903br2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
