\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Jan exports rise, other trade indicators weak. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vithoon Amorn \par \uc2 436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, March 26 (Reuter) - Thai January exports posted moderate year-on-year growth for the first time since last May but trade and current account deficits widened during the month, a finance ministry source said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The source said Thailand's balance of payments also moved to an estimated 26.6 billion baht ($1.02 billion) deficit in February from a 24.7 billion baht surplus in January due to market attacks on the baht in the first half of last month. \par \par \uc2  The senior ministry official said January exports rose about 5.1 percent year-on-year to 115.9 billion baht after falling for seven consecutive months. \par \par \uc2  Exports declined by 0.2 percent for the whole of 1996 after rising 23.6 percent in 1995. \par \par \uc2  The official said more Thai canned seafood, shoes, electrical appliances and furniture were shipped out in January but agricultural commodity exports slowed down. \par \par \uc2  January imports also surged to about 154.9 billion baht as factory demand for petroleum and industrial raw materials escalated, from 139.3 billion baht in December. \par \par \uc2  January imports were at their highest monthly level since May 1996. \par \par \uc2  He said the January trade deficit widened sharply to a higher than expected 39.0 billion baht, a high since April 1996 and against 22.3 billion in December. \par \par \uc2  But the current account deficit, moderated by seasonally higher tourism revenue, widened only slightly to 22.5 billion baht in January from 19.1 billion in December. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's service account, covering mainly tourist and shipping industries, posted a 16.5 billion baht surplus in January, higher than the 3.2 billion baht surplus in December. \par \par \uc2  The official said press reports of a review by Moody's Investors Service of long-term Thai debt ratings and market attacks on the baht emerged during the first half of February, prompting the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in defence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  He said the baht attacks sharply tightened money market liquidity with short-term interbank rates ranging 10-30 percent during February 1-14. \par \par \uc2  The source said, however, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tough stand shored up market sentiment on the baht in the second half of last month, helping the high overnight interbank rate to ease to 8.50-26.00 percent. \par \par \uc2  He said the battered export sector showed no early recovery signs in January with the manufacturing production index estimated to grow only four percent year-on-year, its weakest rate in about 10 years. \par \par \uc2  -- Bangkok newsroom (662)-6520642 (US$1 = 26 baht) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt3q086tz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
